.TH COLON-PATH 7 "@PROJECT_YEAR@" "@PROJECT_VERSION@" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
colon-path \- atomic subpath notation for colon-subdirs tools
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B colon-path
has the form
.BR BASE:LEAF .
The corresponding physical path is
.BR BASE/LEAF .
.I LEAF
is treated as an atomic name: when placing under a destination directory, or
when choosing names stored inside an archive, tools use
.I LEAF
rather than the full physical path.
.PP
The separator is recognized when
.I BASE
contains
.B /
or is
.B .
or
.BR .. .
For local tools
.RB ( colon-mv ,
.BR colon-cp ,
\&...),
a single
.B :
is also accepted when
.I BASE
has no slash (for example
.BR pkg:lib/foo ).
.PP
.BR colon-scp
and
.B colon-rsync
keep the stricter rule so that
.I host:path
and
.I host::module
are left for the underlying tools.
URI schemes are never treated as colon-paths.
.PP
A leading slash on
.I LEAF
is ignored when joining
.RB ( BASE:/LEAF
is the same physical path as
.BR BASE:LEAF ).
.PP
A leading
.B :
with no
.I BASE
is treated as
.BR /
(for example
.BR :foo/bar
→ physical path
.BR /foo/bar ).
.PP
Bash completion for these tools keeps
.B :
inside the current word and completes
.I LEAF
by listing names under
.BR BASE/ .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B a/b:c/d
Physical path
.BR a/b/c/d ;
atomic name
.BR c/d .
.TP
.B ./pkg:lib/foo
Physical path
.BR ./pkg/lib/foo ;
atomic name
.BR lib/foo .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR colon-mv (1),
.BR colon-cp (1),
.BR colon-zip (1),
.BR colon-unzip (1),
.BR colon-rm (1),
.BR colon-rmdir (1),
.BR colon-scp (1),
.BR colon-rsync (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written by @PROJECT_AUTHOR@ <@PROJECT_EMAIL@>.
